quiet.md #30: wrap right-to-left text in reading order

A line is shaped once and its glyphs come back in display order; cutting that
into rows puts the end of an Arabic paragraph on its first row and breaks
inside words. When text with right-to-left characters needs wrapping, take
each character's advance in reading order to find the breaks (with the same
UAX #14 opportunities), shape every row on its own (the platform orders the
row), and lay the rows end to end with a wrap boundary on each row's first
glyph. Rows drop their trailing spaces. Other text keeps upstream's path.

